NAME
    head — Display the beginning of a file, because sometimes you only
    need to see how things start.

SYNOPSIS
    head [OPTIONS] <file>

DESCRIPTION
    The head command displays the first lines of a file.

    By default, it displays the first 10 lines. The number of lines can
    be changed using the -n option.

USAGE
    > head <file>       # Displays the first 10 lines of the file.
    > head -n 5 <file>  # Displays the first 5 lines of the file.
    > head -n 20 <file> # Displays the first 20 lines of the file.

FUN FACTS
    - head only cares about the beginning of the file.
    - The default is 10 lines, because apparently 10 is enough to get the general idea.
    - head is useful for checking a file without reading the whole thing.
    - If you need the end instead, there is another command for that.

EXAMPLES
    > head notes.txt
        Displays the first 10 lines of notes.txt.

    > head -n 5 notes.txt
        Displays the first 5 lines of notes.txt.

    > head -n 25 /home/user/config.txt
        Displays the first 25 lines of config.txt.

SEE ALSO
    help(1), cat(1), tail(1), less(1), man(1)

    Remember, head only gives you the beginning.
    The rest of the file is still waiting.

